<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>FlowX.Redis.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.1.0" />
    <!--
      The container implementation, not just its abstractions: the registration test builds
      a provider and asks which service types AddFlowXRedis actually produced. Asserting on
      the ServiceDescriptor list instead would assert on what was written down rather than
      on what a host resolves. The same reasoning, and the same pin, as FlowX.Postgres.Tests.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../plugins/FlowX.Redis/FlowX.Redis.csproj" />

    <!--
      The host, because the bus trigger is the one thing in this package that only means
      something in a host: a consumer that reads a message and does not start a flow has
      proved nothing about redelivery, acknowledgement or ordering. RedisStreamBusConsumerTests
      drives FlowBusScan over a real Redis, which is the only place the two halves meet.
      The dependency is one-way and stays inside tests/ — no plugin references a host.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Hosting/FlowX.Hosting.csproj" />

    <!--
      The conformance suite is referenced, not copied — and this project is the reason the
      reference is worth anything. ADR-0009 makes the suite the semantic contract for every
      store and docs/17-Plugin-System.md §5 says a third party claims conformance by
      deriving from it; FlowX.Postgres.Tests did that once, and one derivation cannot show
      that the suite is store-independent, only that it is derivable.

      Nothing in tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests changed to make this work. That is the
      finding WP-54 exists to produce: if the suite had needed an edit to accept Redis it
      would have been a suite written against PostgreSQL, and WP-51 would have failed.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Conformance.Tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
